QB.add(45,[{"n":1,"q":"A 6-year-old child presents with pallor, jaundice, and characteristic facial deformities such as frontal bossing and maxillary prominence. Blood tests reveal microcytic anaemia, and Hb electrophoresis shows a significant increase in fetal hemoglobin levels. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Thalassemia"},{"t":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia"},{"t":"Hereditary spherocytosis"},{"t":"Iron deficiency anemia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Thalassemia"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thalassemia presents with severe anemia, characteristic facial deformities, and an increase in fetal hemoglobin (HbF) levels, which is typical of this condition."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thalassemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thalassemia is a genetic disorder affecting hemoglobin production, causing microcytic, hypochromic anemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemoglobin consists of two alpha (a) and two beta (b) chains, and mutations in the genes responsible for these chains lead to alpha or beta thalassemia , inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern. The severity depends on the number of affected globin genes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hemoglobin Structure and Genetics: Hemoglobin (HbA): Made of two a and two b chains."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alpha Globin (a): Four genes located on chromosome 16."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta Globin (b): Two genes located on chromosome 11."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Types and Subtypes of Thalassemia: Alpha Thalassemia (a-thalassemia):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Caused by deletions or mutations in the HBA1 and HBA2 genes on chromosome 16, affecting a chain production. The severity depends on how many of the four a genes are affected:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Silent Carrier (1 gene deletion)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alpha Thalassemia Trait (a-thalassemia minor) (2 gene deletions)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemoglobin H Disease (3 gene deletions)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemoglobin Bart’s Hydrops Fetalis (4 gene deletions)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Beta Thalassemia (b-thalassemia):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Caused by mutations in the HBB gene on chromosome 11, affecting b chain production. The severity depends on whether one or both b genes are mutated:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta Thalassemia Trait (b-thalassemia minor) (1 mutated gene)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta Thalassemia Intermedia (2 mutated genes with partial function)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta Thalassemia Major (b0-thalassemia) (2 mutated genes, with complete absence of b chain production)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptoms of Thalassemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fatigue, weakness, and lethargy (due to anemia)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pallor (pale skin) and jaundice (yellow skin and eyes)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Delayed growth and development in children"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Skeletal deformities (e.g., frontal bossing, depressed nasal bridge, protruding maxilla), \" thalassemic facies\" or \"monkey facies\""},{"k":"img","v":7002},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Splenomegaly (enlarged spleen) and hepatomegaly (enlarged liver)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frequent infections (especially after splenectomy)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron overload (from repeated transfusions , causing heart, liver, and endocrine damage)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (Option B) : This condition typically shows evidence of hemolysis (e.g., elevated reticulocyte count, positive Coombs test) and may not present with the characteristic facial features or elevated HbF levels seen in thalassemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hereditary spherocytosis (Option C) : While hereditary spherocytosis can cause anemia, it is associated with different laboratory findings and does not typically show elevated HbF levels."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Iron deficiency anemia (Option D) : This condition also causes microcytic anemia but would not have elevated fetal hemoglobin levels or the characteristic facial deformities seen in thalassemia."}]},{"n":2,"q":"An 8-month-old baby presents with severe anemia, pallor, and jaundice. The clinical history indicates a similar episode two months prior. An electrophoresis was conducted to differentiate among various forms of thalassemia, and the results are shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Alpha thalassemia trait"},{"t":"Beta thalassemia major"},{"t":"Beta thalassemia minor"},{"t":"Alpha thalassemia major"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Beta thalassemia major"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The presence of significantly elevated HbF and low or absent HbA in the electrophoresis results indicates beta thalassemia major. This form is characterized by severe anemia, pallor, and jaundice in infancy due to ineffective erythropoiesis and hemolysis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Beta thalassemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Beta thalassemia results from mutations in the HBB gene on chromosome 11, which encodes the beta globin chains. Humans have two beta globin genes, and mutations in one or both genes lead to different severities of beta-thalassemia."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Beta Thalassemias Major (Transfusion-depen Intermedia (Non-transfusio Minor (Trait or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"dent) n-dependent) Carrier)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Genotype Homozygous: Variable: Heterozygous:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"bn/bn bn/bn or bn/bn or bn/b bn/b or bn/b bn/bn or bn/b bn/bn"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Laboratory Findings Severe microcytic anemia Moderate microcytic Mild microcytic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"with target cells (Hb 3–4 anemia anemia g/dL)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hemoglobin HbAn (5% or more); HbAn (4% or more); HbAn (4% or Electrophoresis HbF (up to 95%); No HbA HbF (up to 50%) more);"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"HbF (up to 5%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"bn : No beta-globin production"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"bn : Decreased beta-globin production"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Feature Beta Thalassemia Minor Beta Thalassemia Beta Thalassemia Major"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"(Trait) Intermedia (Cooley's Anemia) (Option C ruled out) (Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Genetic One beta-globin gene Both beta-globin genes Both beta-globin genes Mutatio mutated , and the other is mutated but allowed some are severely mutated or n functional beta-chain production absent"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sympto Typically asymptomatic, Pallor, jaundice, delayed Profound fatigue, failure ms with mild fatigue growth, bone deformities to thrive, bone"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"deformities, hepatospleno megaly"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Age of Usually diagnosed Variable; symptoms often Symptoms present within Onset incidentally through arise in childhood the first year of life"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"routine blood tests"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Feature Beta Thalassemia Minor Beta Thalassemia Beta Thalassemia Major"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"(Trait) Intermedia (Cooley's Anemia) (Option C ruled out) (Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Blood Rarely needed Occasional transfusions, Regular, lifelong Transfu particularly during transfusions required sions stress/illness"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Iron Ov Rare May occur if frequent Significant risk due to erload transfusions are given frequent transfusions; iron"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"chelation therapy needed"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Complic None significant Splenomegaly, growth Iron overload complication ations delay, bone changes s (heart, liver damage),"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"severe growth issues"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hb Elec Normal or slight increase Increased HbA2 and Elevated HbF with very trophor in HbA2 possibly HbF low or absent HbA esis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"HPLC Normal hemoglobin profile Abnormal profile with Abnormal profile with high"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"increased HbA2 and/or levels of HbF and very HbF low HbA"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Alpha Thalassemia Trait (Option A):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is characterized by a mild reduction in alpha globin production, typically leading to mild microcytic anemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis usually shows normal levels of HbA, with no significant increase in HbH or Bart's."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This condition is generally asymptomatic and would not typically present with severe anemia or jaundice."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Alpha Thalassemia Major (Option D):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a severe condition caused by the deletion of all four alpha globin genes, leading to profound anemia and typically fetal demise or severe disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis usually shows high levels of hemoglobin Bart's (gamma4), which is incompatible with life after birth."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An 8-month-old would not present with this condition due to its severe and life-threatening nature occurring earlier in life."}],"qi":[6978]},{"n":3,"q":"A community survey revealed a high incidence of thalassemia, leading to the initiation of a screening program using the NESTROFT test. What would be the expected appearance of the NESTROFT test for a negative result?","o":[{"t":"Clear fluid with no visible lines in either tube."},{"t":"Black lines visible in both the test and control tubes."},{"t":"Black line visible in the control tube only."},{"t":"Black line visible in the test tube only."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Black lines visible in both the test and control tubes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The NESTROFT test results show distinct black lines in both the test and control tubes, indicating a negative result. This suggests that no evidence of thalassemia is detected."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Naked Eye Single Tube RBC Osmotic Fragility Test (NESTROFT):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It is a simple and effective screening test used primarily to detect thalassemia and other hemoglobinopathies."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Observation of results:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Test Observation Interpretation Resu lt"},{"k":"img","v":7010},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Nega Indicates no unlysed RBCs present. tive Thalassemia is not detected;"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"osmotic stability is normal."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Distinct black lines visible in both the test and control tubes."},{"k":"img","v":7012},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Posit Suggests the presence of unlysed ive RBCs, indicating osmotic fragility."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"May imply thalassemia or other conditions affecting RBC stability."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Black lines not visible in the test tube, but visible in the control tube."}]},{"n":4,"q":"An X-ray of the skull shows the characteristic findings described below. Which of the following diseases is most likely indicated by these findings?","o":[{"t":"Thalassemia"},{"t":"Multiple Myeloma"},{"t":"Paget's Disease"},{"t":"Histiocytosis X"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Thalassemia"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thalassemia is characterized by abnormal haemoglobin production, leading to compensatory erythropoiesis in the bone marrow. The \" hair-on-end\" appearance in skull X-rays is due to marrow expansion, which is a hallmark of thalassemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"X-ray Skull Findings in Various Pathologies"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Disease X-ray Skull Finding"},{"k":"img","v":6984},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Thalassemia (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"\"Hair-on-end\" appearance due to increased erythropoiesis and skull remodelling."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Multiple \"Punched-out\" lytic lesions in the skull, indicative of Myeloma bone erosion caused by myeloma cells. (Option B) Myeloma Xray Skull\" data-author=\"Ranchod A\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"data-hash=\"10393\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"https://radiopaedia.org/cases/multiple-myel oma-67?lang=gb\" data-tags=\"April2025\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"99TJz3E1744717155.png\" />"},{"k":"img","v":7018},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Paget's Disea se (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Thickened skull bones with a \"cotton wool\" appearance due to disorganized bone remodelling."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Disease X-ray Skull Finding"},{"k":"img","v":7024},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Histiocytosis X (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Lytic bone lesions with a \"punched-out\" appearance, especially in the skull, due to aggressive bone involvement."}],"qi":[6984]},{"n":5,"q":"A 20-year-old man with transfusion-dependent beta-thalassemia is receiving regular packed red blood cell transfusions and oral iron chelation therapy. He seeks additional treatment options to improve his quality of life. Which of the following treatment options is available for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) with a matched sibling donor."},{"t":"Lentiviral gene therapy with autologous CD34+ hematopoietic stem cells."},{"t":"Luspatercept to enhance erythropoiesis and reduce transfusion needs."},{"t":"All of the above"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) All of the above."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT), lentiviral gene therapy, and luspatercept are all aimed at improving the quality of life and reducing the need for frequent transfusions in patients with transfusion-dependent beta-thalassemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of ß Thalassemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For optimal effectiveness, transfusions and iron chelation must be initiated early, remain consistent, and continue throughout life."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood Transfusion: Packed red blood cell transfusion. Transfusion is done every 2-4 weeks. Pretransfusion target hemoglobin concentration: 9-10.5 g/dL. To prevent immunological reactions: Leucoreduced packed red blood cells with a minimum haemoglobin content of 40g should be given. The lifespan of transfused RBC: 60 days."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Packed red blood cell transfusion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transfusion is done every 2-4 weeks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pretransfusion target hemoglobin concentration: 9-10.5 g/dL."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"To prevent immunological reactions: Leucoreduced packed red blood cells with a minimum haemoglobin content of 40g should be given."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The lifespan of transfused RBC: 60 days."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Packed red blood cell transfusion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transfusion is done every 2-4 weeks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pretransfusion target hemoglobin concentration: 9-10.5 g/dL."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"To prevent immunological reactions: Leucoreduced packed red blood cells with a minimum haemoglobin content of 40g should be given."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The lifespan of transfused RBC: 60 days."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron Chelation: 1 unit of transfused blood contains: 200-250 mg of iron. Iron chelation is necessary to prevent toxic iron accumulation from transfusions. Effective in preventing the development of cardiomyopathy and endocrinopathies associated with transfusions. Oral chelators: Deferasirox and deferiprone. Intravenous chelator: Deferoxamine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"1 unit of transfused blood contains: 200-250 mg of iron."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron chelation is necessary to prevent toxic iron accumulation from transfusions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Effective in preventing the development of cardiomyopathy and endocrinopathies associated with transfusions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral chelators: Deferasirox and deferiprone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intravenous chelator: Deferoxamine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"1 unit of transfused blood contains: 200-250 mg of iron."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iron chelation is necessary to prevent toxic iron accumulation from transfusions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Effective in preventing the development of cardiomyopathy and endocrinopathies associated with transfusions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral chelators: Deferasirox and deferiprone."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intravenous chelator: Deferoxamine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation (HSCT): (Option A) HSCT improves quality of life compared to transfusions and chelation. Curative in over 80% of cases with matched sibling donors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HSCT improves quality of life compared to transfusions and chelation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Curative in over 80% of cases with matched sibling donors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HSCT improves quality of life compared to transfusions and chelation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Curative in over 80% of cases with matched sibling donors"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Improving Ineffective Erythropoiesis: (Option C) Luspatercept is approved for treating transfusion-dependent thalassemia. It enhances late-stage erythropoiesis. Reduces transfusion needs by 33%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Luspatercept is approved for treating transfusion-dependent thalassemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It enhances late-stage erythropoiesis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduces transfusion needs by 33%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Luspatercept is approved for treating transfusion-dependent thalassemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It enhances late-stage erythropoiesis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduces transfusion needs by 33%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gene Therapy: (Option B) Lentiviral gene therapy with autologous CD34+ hematopoietic stem cells"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lentiviral gene therapy with autologous CD34+ hematopoietic stem cells"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lentiviral gene therapy with autologous CD34+ hematopoietic stem cells"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Splenectomy: Performed if splenomegaly causes mechanical complications or when there is a high demand for transfusions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Performed if splenomegaly causes mechanical complications or when there is a high demand for transfusions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Performed if splenomegaly causes mechanical complications or when there is a high demand for transfusions."}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 10-year-old girl presents with fatigue, pallor, and recurrent infections. Laboratory tests reveal microcytic hypochromic anaemia (Hb 8 g/dL) and elevated serum ferritin. Haemoglobin electrophoresis shows increased fetal haemoglobin (HbF) and decreased adult haemoglobin (HbA). She is started on blood transfusions every 2-4 weeks. Which of the following is not a complication associated with this condition?","o":[{"t":"Cardiac failure due to iron overload"},{"t":"Yersinia infection from transfusions"},{"t":"Increased risk of hyperthyroidism"},{"t":"Osteoporosis due to chronic anemia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Increased risk of hyperthyroidism"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The combination of microcytic anaemia, elevated ferritin, increased fetal haemoglobin, decreased adult haemoglobin and a history of frequent blood transfusions is characteristic of Beta-thalassemia. They are more likely to develop hypothyroidism rather than hyperthyroidism due to iron deposition in endocrine glands."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Complications of ß Features Thalassemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Growth retardation Usually results from delayed or inadequate transfusions,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"but can also occur in well-transfused children."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Delayed puberty; secondary 50% and 25%, respectively. amenorrhea"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Splenomegaly Can trap 1-40% of red blood cell volume, increasing"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"plasma volume and worsening heart failure. Splenectomy is recommended when transfusion needs rise. Prophylactic penicillin required post-splenectomy."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Complications of ß Features Thalassemia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Heart Due to chronic anaemia, iron toxicity, and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension, heart issues progress to congestive failure and arrhythmias. (Option A) Assessed by T2 weighted cardiac MRI."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Leg ulcers Common in thalassemia intermedia."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hepatic disease Fibrosis progressing to cirrhosis is linked to hepatic iron"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"concentration. Hepatitis contributes to the progression. Monitored by MRI."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lung disease/ Lung fibrosis pulmonary Chronic thromboembolic disease hypertension Restrictive pathophysiology"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Intravascular hemolysis Reduced nitric oxide bioavailability"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Thromboembolism Caused by platelet activation, red cell-endothelial"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"interactions, thrombocytosis, endothelial activation and splenectomy."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Endocrinopathies Bronze diabetes"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Hypothyroidism (vs Option C) Hypoparathyroidism Adrenal insufficiency Hypogonadism Excess iron damages the HPA axis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Bone disease Caused by:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Bone marrow expansion Severe iron loading Hypogonadism Osteoporosis (Option D) Extramedullary hematopoietic masses are common in thalassemia intermedia."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Infections Transfusion-associated, often linked to iron overload,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"increases the risk of: Yersinia infection (Option B) Malaria"}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 6-month-old infant of Southeast Asian descent is brought in for evaluation due to jaundice and failure to thrive. A physical exam reveals hepatosplenomegaly. Laboratory tests show moderate anaemia, with a haemoglobin level of 80 g/L. A peripheral smear is shown below. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?","o":[{"t":"Excess a-globin chain precipitation causing ineffective erythropoiesis"},{"t":"Formation of b4 tetramers, leading to unstable hemoglobin"},{"t":"Increased synthesis of g-globin chains leading to Hb Bart's formation"},{"t":"Reduced b-globin chain synthesis, leading to increased fetal hemoglobin"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Formation of b4 tetramers, leading to unstable haemoglobin"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: In HbH disease, the lack of a-globin chains results in an excess of unpaired b-globin chains . These b-globin chains form b4 tetramers (HbH) , which are unstable and precipitate within red blood cells, forming inclusion bodies that are shown in the peripheral smear. This leads to hemolysis and ineffective erythropoiesis, explaining the infant’s anaemia and clinical presentation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Alpha thalassemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It is an Autosomal recessive inheritance."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It is most common in Southern China and Southeast Asia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Classification :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal individuals have four a-globin genes (two per chromosome 16 )."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The classification depends on the number of functional a-globin genes:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Classification a-globin gene Clinical Features"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"arrangement"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"a-Thalassemia Trait -a/aa, -a/-a, Mild hematologic abnormalities,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"-- /aa, aTa/aa often asymptomatic. ( 1-2 a-globin genes missing)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hemoglobin H (HbH) Disease --/-a, aTa/--, Mild to moderate anaemia, variable"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"a Ta/-a severity depending on genotype. ( 3 a-globin genes missing)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hb Bart’s Hydrops Fetalis --/-- Fatal in utero or shortly after birth."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"( 0 functioning Hydrops fetalis. a -globin genes)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"a-Thalassemia with Intellectual --/aa or --/-a Associated with large deletions and Disability aa/aa rearrangements on chromosome 16 (ATR-16) (ATR-16). (ATR-X) No a-globin gene deletion,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"mutations in the ATRX gene, or X-linked inheritance."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"a-Thalassemia with aa/aa Mutations in ATRX Myelodysplasia (ATMDS) HbH disease-like hematologic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"findings. predominantly in males."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduced a-globin synthesis leads to unpaired globin chains (g-globin in fetuses and b -globin in adults)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Excess g-globin forms g4 (Hb Bart’s) (Option C) , and excess b-globin forms b4 (HbH) ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Both Hb Bart’s and HbH have high oxygen affinity, preventing oxygen release in tissues."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HbH is unstable, causing hemolysis and ineffective erythropoiesis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Golf ball inclusions - Tetramer of beta-globin chains (studded RBC)"},{"k":"img","v":6991},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most sensitive approach to detect HbH involves incubating peripheral blood cells for 1 to 2 hours at 37°C with supravital dyes (brilliant cresyl blue or methyl violet)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These dyes cause the abnormal haemoglobin to precipitate as inclusion bodies ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Excess a-globin chain precipitation causing ineffective erythropoiesis (Option A) occurs in b -thalassemia due to unpaired a-globin chains, not in HbH disease, where b-globin chains are excessive. Reduced b-globin chain synthesis, leading to increased fetal haemoglobin (Option D) describes b -thalassemia, while HbH disease involves deficient a-globin, leading to excess b-globin chain tetramers."}],"qi":[6991]},{"n":8,"q":"A pregnant woman is a silent carrier for a-thalassemia (a-/aa). Her partner has the a-thalassemia trait (aa/--). They are concerned about the risk of their child inheriting a-thalassemia. What is the probability that their child will have Hemoglobin H (HbH) disease?","o":[{"t":"0%"},{"t":"25%"},{"t":"50%"},{"t":"75%"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) 25%"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This couple will have a 25% chance of their child being affected with Hemoglobin H Disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Parent Genotypes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mother (Silent carrier): a-/aa"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Father (a-thalassemia trait): aa/–"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Punnett Square Analysis:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"a- (Mother) aa (Mother)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"aa (Father) a-/aa aa/aa"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"-- (Father) a-/-- aa/--"},{"k":"img","v":7032},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Interpretation of Results:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Alpha Thalassemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Alpha thalassemia results from deletions or mutations in the HBA1 and HBA2 genes on chromosome 16, which encode the alpha globin chains. There are four alpha globin genes, and the severity of the disorder depends on how many of these genes are affected."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Types Affected Genotype Hemoglobin Analysis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Genes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Silent Carrier (Typically 1 out of 4 -a/aa or aa/-a Normal or slightly reduced asymptomatic) genes MCV and MCH Types Affected Genotype Hemoglobin Analysis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Genes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Alpha Thalassemia Trait 2 out of 4 -a/-a (trans) or Reduced MCV, MCH, (Minor) genes --/aa (cis) normal Hb electrophoresis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hemoglobin H Disease 3 out of 4 --/-a Presence of Hb H (b4),"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"genes reduced MCV and MCH,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Hb electrophoresis shows Hb H"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hydrops Fetalis (Hb 4 out of 4 --/-- Presence of Hb Bart's (g4) Bart's) genes"}]},{"n":9,"q":"A newborn is admitted to the neonatal intensive care unit due to respiratory distress and severe jaundice. The infant is lethargic and has poor feeding. Physical examination reveals pallor, marked hepatosplenomegaly, and generalized oedema. Laboratory tests indicate a haemoglobin level of 6 g/dL, elevated indirect bilirubin, and a reticulocyte count of 12%. What is the most likely underlying genetic cause of this condition?","o":[{"t":"Deletion of three a-globin genes"},{"t":"Deletion of all four a-globin genes"},{"t":"Increased b-globin chain synthesis"},{"t":"Mutation in the G6PD gene"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Deletion of all four a-globin genes"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The scenario indicates a-thalassemia due to severe anaemia (Hb: 6 g/dL) from insufficient a -globin production and elevated indirect bilirubin, suggesting hemolysis. Hepatosplenomegaly results from increased erythropoiesis and splenic sequestration. A reticulocyte count of 12% reflects the bone marrow's compensatory response. The rapid onset of symptoms in the newborn aligns with severe a-thalassemia, especially from the deletion of all four a-globin genes ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hb Bart Hydrops Fetalis Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The most severe form of a-thalassemia, caused by the absence of all four a-globin genes (homozygous a 0-thalassemia). Common in Southeast Asia : High frequency due to the prevalence of a0-thalassemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fetuses with a0-thalassemia mainly produce Hb Bart (g4) , which is ineffective for oxygen transport. Small amounts of embryonic haemoglobins (Hbs Portland 1 and 2) allow survival until late pregnancy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Course"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Characterized by severe anaemia (Hb 3-8 g/dL), hepatosplenomegaly, generalized oedema , heart failure, and extramedullary erythropoiesis. Associated congenital abnormalities include skeletal, cardiovascular, and urogenital defects."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hb Bart Hydrops Fetalis Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pregnancy Complications"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Maternal : Preeclampsia, polyhydramnios or oligohydramnios, antepartum haemorrhage. Postpartum : Retained placenta, eclampsia, haemorrhage, anaemia, sepsis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Intrauterine erythrocyte transfusions and hematopoietic stem cell transplantation have uncertain benefits due to developmental risks. Early termination of at-risk pregnancies is advised due to severe complications. Universal prenatal screening programs have been implemented to detect homozygous a-thalassemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Deletion of three a-globin genes (Option A) would lead to Hemoglobin H disease, which typically presents with less severe symptoms than observed in this infant. Increased b-globin chain synthesis (Option C) is seen in b-thalassemia but does not explain the acute symptoms, as the absence of a-globin would lead to the formation of unstable tetramers (b4) rather than healthy haemoglobin."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mutation in the G6PD gene (Option D) causes hemolytic anaemia primarily triggered by oxidative stress, rather than presenting acutely with severe anaemia and jaundice in a newborn."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 5-year-old boy presents with fatigue and intermittent jaundice. His blood film shows spherocytes, and his parents report a family history of similar symptoms. Laboratory tests reveal elevated indirect bilirubin and reticulocytosis. What is the most common underlying defect in this patient’s condition?","o":[{"t":"Deficiency of beta-spectrin"},{"t":"Deficiency of Ankyrin"},{"t":"Deficiency of Protein 4.2"},{"t":"Deficiency of AE1 (Band 3)"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Deficiency of Ankyrin"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ankyrin deficiency is the most common defect in hereditary spherocytosis , accounting for approximately 50% to 70% of cases. Ankyrin is critical for maintaining the connection between the cytoskeletal framework and the lipid bilayer of RBC. Its deficiency leads to the formation of spherocytes, which are less deformable and more prone to splenic destruction, causing hemolysis , consistent with the patient's clinical features."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hereditary Spherocytosis (HS)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"An inherited disorder caused by intrinsic defects in the red blood cell membrane skeleton, leading to spheroid, less deformable red cells vulnerable to splenic sequestration and destruction. 75% of cases are autosomal dominant . Remaining cases often involve severe forms due to compound heterozygosity (inheritance of two different defects)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hereditary Spherocytosis (HS)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Etiopathogenesis Spherocytosis Etiopathogenesis\" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"10398\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/mEFsMIbiKrxPlmLOsDtR1744717931.png\" /> HS is marked by a loss of membrane surface area relative to the intracellular volume, leading to spherical red blood cells with reduced or absent central pallor . Spherocytes have decreased deformability , resulting in their retention and destruction in the spleen , causing hemolysis. spectrin , ankyrin, AE1(band 3) , protein 4.2 that weakens the vertical connections between the cytoskeletal framework and lipid bilayer. The ANK1 gene on chromosome 8p produces Ankyrin . Two proposed mechanisms for membrane loss: In cells with spectrin/ankyrin deficiency . Reduced AE1/protein ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical features"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"60-70% of patients present with moderate disease, typically in infancy or childhood. Common findings in children include: Anaemia may be asymptomatic, Jaundice is usually intermittent and acholuric (unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia). >75% show palpable splenomegaly . Family history."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Complications"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gallbladder Disease : Chronic hemolysis can lead to bilirubinate gallstones . Crisis Events : Hemolytic Crisis : Commonly associated with viral illnesses in childhood; typically mild and self-resolving. Aplastic Crisis : Rare but may cause severe anaemia and require hospitalization, often linked to parvovirus infection. Typically lasts 10–14 days and may reveal undiagnosed HS in asymptomatic patients. Megaloblastic Crisis : Occurs in patients with increased folate needs, preventable by supplementation. Other rare complications may include leg ulcers , chronic dermatitis, and skeletal abnormalities. HS has been associated with increased risks of hematologic malignancies and thrombotic events post-splenectomy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Deficiency of beta-spectrin (Option A) causes Elliptocytosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"AE1 (Band 3) (Option D) is involved in anion exchange and helps maintain cell shape & Protein 4.2 (Option C) maintains red blood cell membrane integrity. However, these deficiencies are less frequent than ankyrin deficiencies in hereditary spherocytosis."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 25-year-old male presents to the emergency department with fatigue and pallor. A complete blood count reveals a haemoglobin level of 100 g/L. A blood film shows erythrocytes with a lack of central pallor, and the mean corpuscular haemoglobin concentration (MCHC) is reported as 38 g/dL. Which of the following laboratory findings would you expect to find in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Positive Direct Coombs Test"},{"t":"Decreased lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) levels"},{"t":"Decreased serum haptoglobin"},{"t":"Decreased reticulocyte count"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Decreased serum haptoglobin"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The presence of spherocytes and elevated MCHC i n the patient's blood film, along with anaemia symptoms, indicates hereditary spherocytosis . Increased hemolysis leads to the release of free haemoglobin into the bloodstream, causing haptoglobin levels to decrease due to its binding and consumption during this process."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Laboratory features of Hereditary Spherocytosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Blood Film"},{"k":"img","v":7038},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Erythrocyte morphology in HS is inconsistent, often showing spherocytes lacking central pallor. Patients with AE1/ BAND3 defects may exhibit \" pincered\" or mushroom-shaped red cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Erythrocyte Indices"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mild to moderate anemia (Hb - 90–120 g/L). Mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration (MCHC) is elevated (>36 g/dL) due to cellular dehydration in about half of the patients. Increased red cell distribution width. Mean corpuscular volume (MCV) is decreased in severe cases."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Markers of Hemolysis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Laboratory features include reticulocytosis (Option D) , elevated lactate dehydrogenase (Option B) , increased urinary and fecal urobilinogen, unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, and decreased serum haptoglobin . Direct Coombs Test - Negative (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"INVESTIGATIONS:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Erythrocyte Fragility Tests"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Spherocytes are osmotically fragile due to decreased surface area relative to volume. The osmotic fragility test measures red cell lysis in hypotonic sodium chloride solutions; spherocytes burst at higher concentrations than normal cells. Other tests, such as glycerol lysis tests , have poor sensitivity."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Eosin 5¢-Maleimide (EMA) Flow Cytometry Test"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"EMA is a fluorescent dye that binds to specific transmembrane proteins (AE1, Rh protein, Rh glycoprotein, CD47). HS patients exhibit decreased fluorescence compared to controls."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Advanced Diagnostic Methods"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sodium Dodecyl Sulfate Polyacrylamide Gel Electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) : SDS-PAGE identifies defective membrane proteins by separating and analyzing their quantities. Molecular Diagnostics : Identify genetic mutations associated with HS, including SPTA1, SPTB, ANK1, SLC4A1, and EPB42. These methods are advanced and often used when other diagnostic tests do not provide a clear result."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic protocol"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Spherocytosis Diagnostic protocol \" data-author=\"\" data-hash=\"10400\" data-license=\"\" data-source=\"\" data-tags=\"April2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/5Dt4b54MZPbdVi XHiHXU1744718092.png\" />"}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 7-year-old boy presents with pallor, jaundice, and a history of recurrent episodes of hemolytic anemia requiring blood transfusions. He is diagnosed with hereditary spherocytosis (HS). His parents are concerned about the repeated need for transfusions and ask about definitive treatment options. What would be the most appropriate next step in managing this patient?","o":[{"t":"Bone marrow transplant"},{"t":"Delaying splenectomy until age 10"},{"t":"Laparoscopic splenectomy"},{"t":"Erythropoietin therapy"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Laparoscopic splenectomy"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Laparoscopic splenectomy is the best treatment for a 7-year-old with hereditary spherocytosis (HS) requiring recurrent transfusions. It improves anemia by prolonging red cell survival, reducing transfusion needs, and preventing complications like iron overload . Since the patient is older than 5 , the risk of post-splenectomy sepsis is lower, making this the ideal surgical approach due to quicker recovery and fewer complications."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of Hereditary Spherocytosis (HS)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Splenectomy as Primary Treatment (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of Hereditary Spherocytosis (HS)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Splenectomy significantly improves anemia by prolonging red cell survival, reducing the need for transfusions, and preventing iron overload and related organ damage. Post splenectomy: Spherocytosis and altered osmotic fragility persist, but reticulocyte counts and erythrocyte lifespan normalize."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Blood Film Changes Post-Splenectomy"},{"k":"img","v":7045},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Features like Howell-Jolly bodies , acanthocytes, target cells, cabot ring and Pappenheimer bodies appear after splenectomy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Indications for Splenectomy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Recommended for patients with severe, transfusion-dependent anemia, growth failure, skeletal changes, leg ulcers, or extramedullary hematopoietic tumours."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Timing of Splenectomy : Should be delayed until age 5–9 if possible (Option B) , or at least age 3, to reduce the risk of post-splenectomy sepsis while balancing the increased risk of gallstones in older children."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Laparoscopic Splenectomy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Preferred method, with benefits like less discomfort, quicker recovery, and shorter hospital stays. Combined laparoscopic splenectomy and cholecystectomy is an option for patients with symptomatic gallstones."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Partial Splenectomy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Used in infants or young children to alleviate anemia while preserving some splenic immune function. It reduces transfusion needs but may eventually require total splenectomy in about half of patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pre- and Post-Splenectomy Care"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Vaccination : Patients should receive vaccines against pneumococcus, Haemophilus influenzae type B, and meningococcus before surgery. Antibiotic Prophylaxis : Use of prophylactic antibiotics post-splenectomy is controversial but recommended by some for at least 5 years or even lifelong. Folic Acid : Continued supplementation (1 mg/day) is recommended to prevent folate deficiency."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bone marrow transplant (Option A) is not indicated in HS, as the condition is caused by a red cell membrane defect, not bone marrow failure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Erythropoietin (Option D) is ineffective in HS, as the issue is not a lack of red cell production but their premature destruction. Thus, it would not address the underlying problem."}]}]);
